WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.550
This video covers the most common pattern in production systems: answering from your own documents.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:11.336
A customer asks your support bot which firmware fixes the dock wake-up bug.

00:00:11.586 --> 00:00:17.774
The bot answers, with total confidence, version 3.2. That version does not exist.

00:00:18.024 --> 00:00:26.313
The right answer, 2.1.4, was sitting in your knowledge base the whole time. This video is about making the model look it up and prove it.

00:00:26.563 --> 00:00:36.884
Model: A trained file of numbers plus the math that uses them to turn an input into an output. For text, the input is your words and the output is a guess at the next word. Like: A compiled lookup engine: firmware image (the numbers) plus the code that reads it.

00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:39.119
Think of an exam.

00:00:39.519 --> 00:00:47.262
Without retrieval, the model answers from memory, which may be outdated, or may never have contained your internal documents at all.

00:00:47.662 --> 00:00:57.937
With retrieval augmented generation, or RAG, your code first finds the most relevant passages in your own documents, and puts them in the prompt. It becomes an open book exam.

00:00:58.337 --> 00:01:15.091
RAG: Retrieval-augmented generation: before asking the model, search your own documents for the relevant passages and paste them into the prompt so it answers from them. Like: An open-book exam: first find the right pages, then write the answer from them.

00:01:15.391 --> 00:01:26.478
Prompt: Everything you send the model in one call: instructions, examples, data and the question. Like: The full test vector you feed a device under test.

00:01:26.778 --> 00:01:31.364
And you require a citation for every claim, so anyone can check the page.

00:01:31.764 --> 00:01:43.606
Citation: An answer that names which retrieved chunk each claim came from, so a person or a program can verify it. Like: A footnote that points to the exact datasheet page.

00:01:44.706 --> 00:01:47.992
Here is the full pipeline, which you build in lab six.

00:01:48.392 --> 00:01:53.756
Split documents into chunks, short passages with a little overlap so no fact is cut in half.

00:01:54.156 --> 00:02:09.759
Chunking: Cutting documents into smaller passages (chunks) before indexing, so a search returns just the relevant part rather than a whole manual. Like: Splitting a firmware image into flash pages: small units you can find and fetch individually.

00:02:10.059 --> 00:02:23.887
Chunk overlap: Repeating a little text at the end of one chunk and the start of the next, so a sentence cut at a boundary still appears whole in one of them. Like: Overlapping photos in a panorama so nothing falls in the seam.

00:02:24.187 --> 00:02:28.924
Index every chunk twice: once for keyword search, once as embedding vectors.

00:02:29.324 --> 00:02:46.240
BM25 (keyword search): The classic keyword-search ranking formula: scores a document higher when it contains the query words, especially rare ones, without rewarding endless repetition. Like: grep with judgement: rare words like an error code count far more than "the".

00:02:46.540 --> 00:03:02.167
Embedding: A list of numbers (a vector) that represents the meaning of a piece of text, made so that texts with similar meaning get nearby vectors. Like: GPS coordinates for meaning: "puppy" and "dog" are neighbours, "invoice" is in another city.

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:20.218
Vector: An ordered list of numbers, such as [0.12, -0.80, 0.33]. In this course, almost always an embedding. Like: A fixed-size array of floats, exactly like float v[1536] in C.

00:03:20.518 --> 00:03:23.908
For each question, retrieve candidates with both methods.

00:03:24.308 --> 00:03:33.689
Merge the two ranked lists. A simple, robust way is reciprocal rank fusion, which scores each chunk by its positions in the lists, not by raw scores.

00:03:34.089 --> 00:03:53.431
RRF: Reciprocal rank fusion: a simple way to merge ranked lists. Each document scores 1/(k + its rank) in each list (k is often 60); add the scores and sort. Like: Merging two judges' rankings by giving points for placement, not raw scores they cannot compare.

00:03:53.731 --> 00:03:58.875
Rerank the shortlist with a slower model that reads the question and each passage together.

00:03:59.275 --> 00:04:15.912
Reranker: A second, slower but sharper model that re-scores the top results of a search by reading the query and each passage together, then re-orders them. Like: A first-pass resume filter, then a hiring manager who reads the shortlisted ones carefully.

00:04:16.212 --> 00:04:27.636
Finally, the model answers using only those passages, citing them, and your code checks that every citation points to a real passage. If not, the answer becomes: I don't know.

00:04:28.836 --> 00:04:31.541
How do you know any of this works? You measure.

00:04:31.941 --> 00:04:36.341
Build a set of real questions where you know which document holds the answer.

00:04:36.741 --> 00:04:41.049
Recall at k asks: did the right document make the top k results?

00:04:41.449 --> 00:05:03.287
Recall@k / MRR: Retrieval metrics. Recall@k: of the passages that should be found, what fraction appear in the top k. MRR (mean reciprocal rank): on average, how high the first correct passage ranks (1 for first place, 1/2 for second...). Like: Did the right part show up in the first page of catalogue results, and how close to the top?

00:05:03.587 --> 00:05:13.258
Top-k: Keep only the k best results, for example the 5 highest-scoring chunks. Like: "Show me the top 5" on any leaderboard.

00:05:13.558 --> 00:05:17.935
M R R, mean reciprocal rank, asks how close to the top it was.

00:05:18.335 --> 00:05:30.502
Then change one thing at a time, such as chunk size or adding the reranker, and measure again. In the lab's small example, hybrid search lifts recall at 3 from 0.75 to 1.0.

00:05:30.902 --> 00:05:48.248
Hybrid search: Running keyword search and semantic search together and merging the two ranked lists, so you catch both exact identifiers and paraphrases. Like: Asking both the parts catalogue (exact part numbers) and the experienced tech (meaning), then combining their answers.

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:56.174
Question for you: Your RAG bot gives wrong answers. How do you tell whether retrieval or generation is at fault?

00:06:02.474 --> 00:06:17.498
Answer: Look at what was retrieved for the failing questions. If the right passage was not in the top results, fix retrieval and measure recall at k. If it was there and the answer still went wrong, fix the prompt, the model, or the citation check.

00:06:18.598 --> 00:06:19.248
Recap.

00:06:19.648 --> 00:06:23.967
RAG means finding the right passages first, then answering only from them.

00:06:24.367 --> 00:06:27.130
Hybrid search combines keywords and vectors.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:30.885
Rerank, cite, and verify the citations in code.

00:06:31.285 --> 00:06:34.385
And measure retrieval with recall at k and M R R.
